\subsection{Phase Control and Real-Time Stabilization}
Monitoring the projected centrifuge spectrum and fitting to eq. \eqnref{eq:spectral_intensity_common} lends itself to several feedback loops: one could use the visibility $V$ to stabilize the arm powers by rotating a quarter-wave plate, or tune the centrifuge automatically to a chosen setpoint by using the reconstructed FWHM of $f_\mathrm{cfg}$ and translating the stages that set the chirp and the delay. The largest benefit, however, is that the same fit lets us actively stabilize the centrifuge phase.

Thermal expansion and vibrations change the phase of the centrifuge and thus the phase of the spectrum oscillations (eq. \eqnref{eq:spectrum_phase}). Compensating for vibrations is not possible with our setup, due to limitations set by the reaction time of the components. Phase drift that occurs on a minute timescale, however, can easily be compensated. In theory, thermal expansion can alter the constant, linear and quadratic coefficients in eq. \eqnref{eq:spectrum_phase} all at once. Because the path length and chirp difference introduced by any reasonable expansion do not significantly change $\Theta_1$ and $\Theta_2$, it is enough to correct for a constant phase. When fitting the recorded spectrum to eq. \eqnref{eq:spectral_intensity_common} with $\Theta_0$ being the only free variable, one can calculate the change
\begin{equation}
    \delta\Theta_0
=
\Theta_0-\Theta_{0,\mathrm{set}}
\end{equation}
which is used as the error signal for active phase correction.

The correction is applied by rotating a half-wave plate (see \figref{setup_fig}, component \textbf{2}). A rotation of the half-wave plate by an angle \(\delta\gamma\) adds opposite optical phases to the two circular components\footnote{The Jones matrix for a HWP rotated by $\gamma$ is $J_{\mathrm{HWP}}(\gamma)
=
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos(2\gamma) & \sin(2\gamma) \\
\sin(2\gamma) & -\cos(2\gamma)
\end{pmatrix}$. Applying this matrix to the circular polarization basis vector (compare \eqnref{eq:circular_fields}) gives: $J_{\mathrm{HWP}}(\gamma)\,\vec e_R
=
e^{i2\gamma}\vec e_L$ and $J_{\mathrm{HWP}}(\gamma)\,\vec e_L
=
e^{-i2\gamma}\vec e_R$.}, 
\begin{equation}
    \varphi_{R0}\rightarrow \varphi_{R0}+2\delta\gamma,
\qquad
\varphi_{L0}\rightarrow \varphi_{L0}-2\delta\gamma,
\end{equation}
up to an overall sign convention. Hence, the relative phase entering the interference term changes by
\begin{equation}
    \delta\Theta_0
=
\delta(\varphi_{R0}-\varphi_{L0})
=
4\delta\gamma.
\end{equation}
The required wave-plate correction is therefore
\begin{equation}
    \delta\gamma
=
-\frac{\delta\Theta_0}{4},
\end{equation}
where the sign is chosen according to the experimentally calibrated response.
